# The script `mise run ffi-test` has the editor's headless mode run against
# the config in `config/`, which keeps the default layout and binds `C-c s`
# to split the window and `C-c o` to focus the next one. It shows two
# windows on one buffer keeping a cursor and a scroll anchor each, and an
# edit in one showing in both. What it prints must be exactly `expected`.
# It touches no file.

reload
settle

# A frame of 640 by 120, in the recorder's cells of 8 by 16: a window shows
# only a few rows of text, so a buffer of twelve lines is taller than it.
resize 640 120

# Twelve lines, `hello` then `02` to `12`, typed in insert state; `<Escape>`
# returns to normal state and `g g` takes the cursor back to the first
# line, where the view's anchor follows it.
key i KeyI -
commit hello
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 02
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 03
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 04
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 05
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 06
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 07
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 08
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 09
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 10
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 11
key <Enter> Enter -
commit 12
key <Escape> Escape -
key g KeyG -
key g KeyG -

# `C-c s` splits the window: view 1 is beside view 0, on the same buffer,
# with copies of its markers, and the focus stays on view 0.
key c KeyC control
key s KeyS -
scene

# `C-c o` focuses view 1, and `G` takes its cursor to the last line, which
# its anchor follows down; view 0 stays at the top. `C-c o` focuses view 0
# again, and `A` appends `,world` at the end of its line.
key c KeyC control
key o KeyO -
key G KeyG shift
key c KeyC control
key o KeyO -
key A KeyA shift
commit ,world
key <Escape> Escape -
scene
